> Exhaust a finite key census, without timing claims

For each odd-number array below, the program queries every integer from −1
through 2n, including values before, between and after stored keys. It reports
the largest actual read count, the schedule bound and the above-all count.
Square lengths make the upper bound attainable at the last key. No external
dataset is needed for these controlled search questions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_search_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/jump_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <limits>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, worst, above;
    std::print("| n | exact block size | blocks | measured maximum reads | schedule bound | above-all reads " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n = 16; n <= 4096; n *= 4) {
        std::vector<int> a(n);
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            a[i] = 2 * i + 1;
        std::size_t maximum = 0;
        for (int key = -1; key <= int(2 * n); ++key)
            maximum =
                std::max(maximum, dm::algorithms::jump_search(a, key).probes);
        const auto s = dm::algorithms::jump_search_schedule(n);
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::jump_search(a, int(2 * n));
        if (maximum > s.max_probes)
            throw std::runtime_error("read bound");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, s.block_size,
                   s.block_count, maximum, s.max_probes, r.probes);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        worst.push_back(std::log2(maximum));
        above.push_back(std::log2(r.probes));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, worst, "maximum", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, worst, "",
                                                         {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(
        x, above, "above all", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, above, "",
                                                        {220, 38, 38}, 4).title(
        "Measured read census on square input lengths").x_label(
        "log2(input length)").y_label("log2(actual logical reads)").y_limits(
        1, 9).save_svg("build/jump_search_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | exact block size | blocks | measured maximum reads | schedule bound | above-all reads |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 16 | 4 | 4 | 8 | 8 | 4 |
| 64 | 8 | 8 | 16 | 16 | 8 |
| 256 | 16 | 16 | 32 | 32 | 16 |
| 1024 | 32 | 32 | 64 | 64 | 32 |
| 4096 | 64 | 64 | 128 | 128 | 64 |
\image(figures/jump_search_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Finite key censuses show the expected square-root read growth.)
\alttext(Worst-case measured reads are twice the square-root block size, while above-all queries read only the block endpoints.)

Lengths must fit ptrdiff_t before signed result indices are produced. Clipped
endpoint updates add at most the remaining length, avoiding wrap. The schedule
can also be inspected for virtual large lengths without constructing an array.
Exact scheduling may differ from floating sqrt rounding near very large
perfect-square boundaries; ordinary result and logical-probe compatibility were
checked with the frozen original kernel, while independent records checked
branch order.

An independent Python schedule/branch oracle checked 1,000 sorted arrays and
4,293 actual probe records. The frozen original kernel matched 104,000 ordinary
queries exactly, including first-match answers and logical probe counts. Native
checks also cover virtual wide schedules, quotas and duplicates. The shared
ten-report build passed 131 native tests, 38 directly instrumented
address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public examples,
documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten representative
clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs executed successfully and
their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.
